title: Energy & Forces
subject: Physics
level: GCSE
emoji: ⚡
description: Energy stores, conservation, motion and Newton's laws.
---
What is the law of conservation of energy? :: Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred or dissipated.
Name the eight energy stores. :: Kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, nuclear, magnetic, electrostatic.
Kinetic energy formula? :: KE = ½ × mass × speed² (J)
Gravitational potential energy formula? :: GPE = mass × gravitational field strength × height
Elastic potential energy formula? :: Ee = ½ × spring constant × extension²
Power formula? :: Power = energy transferred ÷ time (watts)
Efficiency formula? :: Efficiency = useful energy output ÷ total energy input
What is Earth's gravitational field strength? :: About 9.8 N/kg (≈ 10 N/kg).
Weight formula? :: Weight = mass × gravitational field strength (W = mg)
Difference between mass and weight? :: Mass is the amount of matter (kg); weight is the force of gravity on it (N).
Newton's first law? :: An object stays at rest or at constant velocity unless acted on by a resultant force.
Newton's second law? :: Resultant force = mass × acceleration (F = ma).
Newton's third law? :: Every action has an equal and opposite reaction force.
Speed formula? :: Speed = distance ÷ time
Acceleration formula? :: a = (v − u) ÷ t
What does the gradient of a distance–time graph show? :: Speed.
What does the gradient of a velocity–time graph show? :: Acceleration.
What does the area under a velocity–time graph show? :: Distance travelled.
What is terminal velocity? :: Constant speed reached when drag force equals the driving force (weight).
Stopping distance equals what? :: Thinking distance + braking distance.
Hooke's law? :: Force = spring constant × extension (F = ke), within the limit of proportionality.
Momentum formula? :: Momentum = mass × velocity (kg m/s)
Pressure formula? :: Pressure = force ÷ area
